        "A full census has big strengths. Because it aims to include everyone, it gives the most complete picture of a population — its size, ages, where people live, languages and education — right down to small areas. This makes it ideal for planning schools, hospitals and HDB towns, and because it is repeated every 10 years (Singapore's most recent was 2020) it shows long-term trends such as ageing.\n\nHowever, a full census also has weaknesses. It is very expensive and takes a long time to organise and process, so it is held only once a decade and the data goes out of date. Some groups are hard to count, such as people without a fixed address, and some people give wrong answers.\n\nOther methods can be better for some purposes. Sample surveys are cheaper and quicker, so they can be done more often — Singapore runs a General Household Survey in the middle of each decade. Government registers of births, deaths and addresses are updated continuously. In fact, since 2000 Singapore's census has been register-based: it combines administrative records with a large sample survey rather than visiting every household, which reduces cost and the burden on households.\n\nOverall, I only partly agree. A census is the best single source for a complete, detailed snapshot, but it is not the best way on its own. The most effective approach, as Singapore shows, combines a census with registers and regular surveys to keep data both complete and up to date.",
